(D7 — Discrepancy 7 repro: `Return a buffer, "ABC".` — a text literal in
 the return expression of a buffer-returning function — yields an EMPTY
 buffer at the call site, size 0, printing nothing. The identical text in
 a buffer DECLARATION (`a buffer called direct is "ABC".`, the first two
 lines of output) yields size 3, and a function that builds a buffer
 variable that way and returns THAT also yields size 3 (FUN-23). So the
 text-to-buffer conversion the declaration form performs is not performed
 in the return expression, and no diagnostic says so.
 The strongest pro-compiler reading: `Return a buffer, <text literal>` is
 a type mismatch the analyzer chose to accept rather than reject, and the
 manual never promises a text literal converts to a buffer anywhere
 except in a declaration's initializer. On that reading this is a missing
 diagnostic rather than a wrong answer — but it is silent either way.
 Add ONE more string-initialised buffer anywhere in the program — top
 level or inside a function body — and it stops being silent and starts
 segfaulting. See Discrepancy 8 and D8.vox.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D7.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   3
   ABC
   0
)
